<p>The re-encoding tax is more than computational waste — it architecturally prevents the model from reasoning about its own outputs. If you ask "what color is the shirt you just drew?", the model looks at a fresh encoding of the rendered pixels, not the discrete tokens it produced. UniAR eliminates this gap by using a <strong>single visual tokenizer</strong> whose tokens are equally valid for understanding and generation.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══ §02 BSQ ═══ -->
<section id="s-bsq" data-code="bsq">
<div class="section-no">02 — BSQ: 2⁶⁴ Codes Without a Codebook</div>
<h2>Binary Spherical Quantization: exponential vocabulary at minimal cost</h2>

<p>Traditional visual tokenizers — VQ-VAEs and their descendants — maintain an explicit codebook: a lookup table of N learned embedding vectors. To quantize a feature, you find the nearest codebook entry. Scaling the vocabulary means growing this table, which grows memory and compute linearly.</p>

<p>UniAR uses Binary Spherical Quantization (BSQ). Instead of searching a codebook, BSQ maps each visual feature to a <strong>64-bit binary vector</strong> — a sequence of 64 zeros and ones. Each unique bit pattern is a distinct code. With 64 bits, there are 2⁶⁴ ≈ 18.4 <em>quintillion</em> possible codes — with no explicit lookup table, no embedding matrix, just the bit vector itself.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">vocabulary scale: bit dimension d → code count 2^d</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a dimension to explore</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <span class="ctrl-label">BSQ bits:</span>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-bsq="4">d=4</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-bsq="8">d=8</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-bsq="16">d=16</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-bsq="32">d=32</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" data-bsq="64">d=64 (UniAR)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="bsq-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="bsq-info" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2)"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The quantization process is elegant: a small MLP projects the visual feature into d-dimensional space on a hypersphere, then each dimension is thresholded to 0 or 1. No nearest-neighbour search, no codebook update — just a forward pass and a binary comparison. The soft entropy loss encourages uniform bit usage, preventing codebook collapse.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">BSQ quantization</div>
  <span class="math-var">v</span> = Encoder(<span class="math-var">x</span>)  <span class="math-comment">↖ raw ViT feature (float)</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">u</span> = BSQ(MLP<sub>in</sub>(<span class="math-var">v</span>))  <span class="math-comment">↖ binary vector u ∈ {0,1}^64</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">v'</span> = MLP<sub>out</sub>(<span class="math-var">u</span>)  <span class="math-comment">↖ reconstructed feature from bits</span><br>
  ℒ = ℒ<sub>CE</sub> + λ·ℒ<sub>BSQ</sub>  <span class="math-comment">↖ train with LMM cross-entropy + BSQ entropy</span>
</div>

<p>Critically, the same binary token indexes both the understanding decoder (an LLM head that reads u to predict the next word) and the generation path (the AR model outputs u to describe what pixel region should appear). One integer key opens two very different doors.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══ §03 MULTI-LEVEL ═══ -->
<section id="s-multilevel" data-code="multilevel">
<div class="section-no">03 — Multi-Level Features</div>
<h2>Shallow = textures. Deep = semantics. Fuse both.</h2>

<p>Even with BSQ, there's a fundamental tension. The final layer of a contrastively-trained ViT like SigLIP is excellent for semantics — it knows that "Siamese cat" and "kitten" are similar concepts — but it has discarded the fine pixel detail needed for high-fidelity generation. Shallow layers preserve pixel-level texture and color but lack categorical understanding.</p>

<p>UniAR resolves this with <strong>multi-level feature fusion</strong>: instead of quantizing only the final ViT layer, it fuses features from 4 intermediate layers before applying BSQ. The shallow layers contribute high-frequency texture; the deep layers contribute semantic structure; together they form a representation that serves both tasks.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">feature reconstruction quality: shallow vs deep vs multi-level</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-level="shallow">Shallow only</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-level="deep">Deep only</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" data-level="multi">Multi-level (UniAR)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="level-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="level-desc" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.8rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The paper shows this concretely (Figure 3): conditioning the DiT decoder on shallow features preserves fine-grained text characters (e.g., "HELLO" rendered pixel-for-pixel) but loses object-level structure. Deep features correctly identify object categories but smear fine details. Multi-level conditioning recovers both — and since the tokenizer is trained with LMM cross-entropy rather than reconstruction loss, this dual fidelity emerges as a side effect of learning to understand images, not as an explicit objective.</p>

<h3>DeepStack: tapping four ViT layers</h3>
<p>UniAR implements multi-level fusion via <strong>DeepStack</strong>: features from 4 ViT layers (one shallow, two intermediate, one final) are each passed through BSQ independently, yielding 4 separate 64-bit vectors per spatial position. These 4 × 64-bit vectors form the complete representation at each patch location — jointly encoding both low-level texture and high-level semantics.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══ §04 PARALLEL PREDICTION ═══ -->
<section id="s-parallel" data-code="parallel">
<div class="section-no">04 — Parallel Bitwise Prediction</div>
<h2>32× sequence compression via spatial + level parallelism</h2>

<p>Multi-level BSQ makes each image token information-rich — but it multiplies the sequence length. A 512×512 image has 1024 ViT patches at 16px patch size, each with 4 BSQ vectors: 4096 discrete tokens to predict sequentially. At typical autoregressive speeds, this makes generation prohibitively slow.</p>

<p>UniAR introduces <strong>parallel bitwise prediction</strong>: instead of predicting one BSQ token at a time, the model predicts a <em>group</em> of 16 BSQ tokens simultaneously — a 2×2 spatial block across all 4 ViT levels. The spatial merger first combines each 2×2 patch neighbourhood into one visual position (4 spatial → 1), and the prediction head outputs 16 × 64 = 1024 bits per AR step. The result: a 512×512 image needs only 256 autoregressive steps, achieving a 32× compression ratio.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">parallel bitwise prediction — step through the mechanism</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">use Prev / Next</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Image → ViT patches</div>
          <div class="step-desc">A 512×512 image is divided into 32×32 = 1024 non-overlapping patches at 16px each. Each patch independently produces a float feature vector from the ViT.</div>
          <div class="step-code">patches: 32×32 = 1024  ·  features: R^d per patch</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">BSQ at 4 ViT levels</div>
          <div class="step-desc">DeepStack taps 4 intermediate ViT layers. Each layer's features at each patch are independently BSQ-quantized to a 64-bit binary vector, yielding 4 vectors per patch.</div>
          <div class="step-code">per patch: 4 layers × 64 bits = 256 bits</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">2×2 spatial merger</div>
          <div class="step-desc">A spatial merger aggregates each 2×2 neighbourhood of patches into a single visual token position. This reduces 1024 spatial positions to 256 merged positions — a 4× spatial compression before AR prediction.</div>
          <div class="step-code">1024 patches → 256 merged positions (2×2 → 1)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Parallel group prediction</div>
          <div class="step-desc">At each AR step, the LLM predicts the next merged position — which covers 4 spatial patches × 4 ViT levels = 16 BSQ vectors = 1024 bits, all at once. A single prediction head outputs this directly.</div>
          <div class="step-code">output dim = 2 × 64 × (4 spatial × 4 levels) = 2048</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">256 steps for 512×512</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Total AR steps: 256 merged positions (one per step). Compare to a naive serial approach: 1024 patches × 4 levels = 4096 steps. Parallel bitwise prediction delivers 16× fewer steps, 32× overall compression counting both spatial and level grouping.</div>
          <div class="step-code">256 steps vs 4096 serial  →  32× compression</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
