<p>The efficiency gain translates directly to training speed: discrete token prediction with this compression runs in <strong>24.5 seconds per iteration</strong> versus 35.4 s/iter for continuous-token approaches — a 31% speedup before any quality comparison.</p>

<p>A further trick makes high-quality generation stable: during pretraining, UniAR randomly flips a small fraction of the BSQ bits in the ground-truth input (the "teacher forcing" sequence). This simulates the accumulated errors that appear at inference time when the model reads its own imperfect predictions. Without this perturbation, the model degrades rapidly at sampling temperatures above 0.3; with it, coherent outputs persist even at temperature 1.0.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══ §05 SHARED CONTEXT ═══ -->
<section id="s-shared" data-code="shared">
<div class="section-no">05 — Shared Context Emerges</div>
<h2>Generate then immediately understand — in the same context</h2>

<p>The payoff of a unified tokenizer is an emergent capability that UniAR's training data never explicitly taught. Because the visual BSQ tokens produced during generation are the same tokens the understanding path expects, the model can immediately ask and answer questions about the content it just generated — without any re-encoding step, simply by appending text tokens to the same context window.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">interleaved generate → understand in shared context</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Replay to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="shared-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="margin-top:.75rem">
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="shared-replay">↺ Replay</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The figure above mirrors a concrete experiment from the paper (Figure 5). Given the prompt <em>"a beautiful vase placing on a table, and pictures hanging on the wall"</em>, UniAR generates an image and immediately responds to follow-up questions like "How many pictures are hanging on the wall?" with the correct answer "Two" — using the <em>generated BSQ tokens</em> directly, not a re-encoded rendering.</p>

<p>Models like BAGEL and Janus-Pro cannot do this: their VQ-VAE generation tokens are structurally incompatible with their semantic understanding ViT, so any question about a generated image requires rendering the pixels and re-encoding through a second tokenizer. UniAR's training data contained no interleaved generate-then-understand dialogues — the shared context is entirely emergent from the unified representation.</p>

<h3>Three-stage training</h3>
<p>UniAR is trained in sequence: <strong>pretraining</strong> (800B tokens at 8K context + 200B at 32K), <strong>supervised fine-tuning</strong> (50B tokens in ChatML format), and <strong>reinforcement learning</strong> with GRPO. The RL stage uses multiple rewards: image quality (HPSv2, UnifiedReward), text rendering accuracy (PaddleOCR edit distance), and instruction following (object detector). RL improves text rendering from 71.1 → 87.3 on OneIG-EN in just 600 steps — 500 at 512px resolution plus 100 at 960px.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══ §06 RESULTS ═══ -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>State-of-the-art text rendering, competitive generation and understanding</h2>

<p>UniAR is evaluated across three dimensions: instruction-following image generation (GenEval), text rendering (OneIG-Bench, LongText-Bench), image editing (ImgEdit-Bench), and multimodal understanding (OCRBench, DocVQA, and others). In each, it is compared against both generation-only models (FLUX, DALLE-3) and unified models (BAGEL, X-Omni, Janus-Pro, OmniGen2).</p>

